// wasm3 late-lower rules: run after the main lower pass.
//
// By this point the main-pass FieldGet/FieldSet rules have folded every
// Load/Store directly through a &struct.field OffPtr into struct.get/
// struct.set. The OffPtrs that remain are the ones used as pointer
// VALUES — &container.field whose address escapes or crosses a function
// boundary (e.g. &x.value passed to atomic.Store64). For an i64-class
// scalar field, that pointer is represented as a $go.ptr.i64 accessor-
// pair fat pointer so a generic callee can dereference it via call_ref
// without the container's static type (doc/wasm3-pointer-cutover).
//
// MakeFieldPtr carries the container struct type (aux) and the field
// byte offset (auxint); codegen emits struct.new $go.ptr.i64 with
// ref.func get/set accessors generated for (struct, field).
(OffPtr <t> [off] base) && base.Type.IsPtr() && base.Type.Elem() != nil && base.Type.Elem().IsStruct() && wasm3IsFieldInteriorPtr(t) => (MakeFieldPtr <t> {base.Type.Elem()} [off] base)

// An escaping &container.embeddedStruct at offset 0 is the same boxed
// object reinterpreted as the embedded (prefix) struct type. Go C-style
// struct embedding lays the embedded struct's fields first, and wasm3
// flattens embedded struct fields (wasmtype.go lowerFields), so the
// embedded struct is the exact prefix of the container's wasm struct.
// The "address" is therefore the container ref itself — pointer locals
// are anyref and the use site ref.casts to the needed type. This is the
// shape produced by promoted-method wrappers, e.g. internal/abi's
// (*structTypeUncommon).Elem calling the embedded Type's Elem.
(OffPtr <t> [0] base) && base.Type.IsPtr() && base.Type.Elem() != nil && base.Type.Elem().IsStruct() && t.Elem() != nil && t.Elem().IsStruct() => base
